name: exit status and the special variables
boss: AndOr
flavor: Exit zero and you pass. Anything else is mine.
brief: Every command returns a number. Zero means success; anything else is a
brief: failure the command chose. $? holds the last one.
brief:
brief: That number is what && and || branch on. cmd1 && cmd2 runs the second
brief: only if the first succeeded; cmd1 || cmd2 only if it failed. This is
brief: the smallest conditional the shell has, and the most used.
brief:
brief: The other specials: $$ this shell's pid, $! the last background pid,
brief: $# how many arguments, $@ all of them, $0 the name it was invoked as.
---
q: print the exit status of the command you just ran
a: true; echo $?
out: ^0$
ref: sh
teach: sh
---
q: run false and then show its exit status
a: false; echo $?
out: ^1$
ref: false
teach: false
---
q: run true and confirm its exit status is zero
a: true; echo $?
out: ^0$
ref: true
teach: true
---
q: print %word% only if %log% contains %sev%
a: grep -q %sev% %log% && echo %word%
out: %word%
ref: grep
teach: grep -q
---
q: print "missing" only if %log% does NOT contain the word nonexistent
a: grep -q nonexistent %log% || echo missing
out: ^missing$
ref: grep
teach: grep -q
---
q: print found if %sev% is in %log%, and missing if it is not, in one line
a: grep -q %sev% %log% && echo found || echo missing
out: ^found$
ref: grep
teach: grep -q
---
q: print this shell's own process id
a: echo $$
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: sh
teach: sh
---
q: start sleep 1 in the background and print the pid it was given
a: sleep 1 & echo $!
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: sleep
teach: sleep
---
q: exit the shell with status 0
a: exit 0
ref: exit
teach: exit
---
q: exit the shell with status 3
a: exit 3
ref: exit
teach: exit
hint: any number 0-255; zero means success and everything else is yours to define
---
q: invert an exit status -- succeed when grep finds nothing in %log%
a: ! grep -q nonexistent %log%
ref: grep
teach: grep -q
hint: a leading ! flips the status
---
q: make the shell stop at the first command that fails
a: set -e
ref: set
teach: set -e
---
q: make the shell treat an unset variable as an error rather than an empty string
a: set -u
ref: set
teach: set -u
---
q: make the shell print each command before running it
a: set -x
ref: set
teach: set -x
hint: this is the shell debugger, and it is usually enough
---
q: turn command tracing back off
a: set +x
ref: set
teach: set
---
q: show the shell's current option settings
a: set -o
ref: set
teach: set
